```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule, NgForm } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'profile-form',
  imports: [FormsModule],
  template: `
    <form #form="ngForm" (ngSubmit)="save(form)">
      <label>Email <input name="email" type="email"
        [(ngModel)]="profile.email" required></label>
      <label>Role
        <select name="role" [(ngModel)]="profile.role">
          <option value="learner">Learner</option>
          <option value="mentor">Mentor</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <button type="submit" [disabled]="form.invalid">Save profile</button>
    </form>
    @if (saved) { <p>Profile saved.</p> }
  `,
})
class ProfileForm {
  profile = { email: '', role: 'learner' };
  saved = false;
  save(form: NgForm) { if (form.valid) this.saved = true; }
}
```

Template-driven validation attaches validators in the template and reads their results through `NgModel` and `NgForm`. Built-in attributes such as `required`, `minlength`, `maxlength`, and `pattern` are recognised by Angular's validation directives. A custom directive can provide a domain-specific validator when the rule is reusable.

The field can be exported with `#name="ngModel"`. Its `errors` object records which rules failed, while `dirty` and `touched` describe user interaction. The form exposes combined validity and a `submitted` flag. This allows an interface to avoid showing errors before the learner has had a chance to type.

For example:

```typescript
template: `<form #form="ngForm">
  <input name="name" [(ngModel)]="model.name"
         required minlength="2" #name="ngModel">
  @if (name.invalid && (name.touched || form.submitted)) {
    @if (name.errors?.['required']) { <p>Name is required.</p> }
    @if (name.errors?.['minlength']) { <p>Use at least two characters.</p> }
  }
</form>`
```

Messages should say how to fix the value and be associated with the control for accessibility, for example with `aria-describedby`. Disabling submit can be helpful, but it should not be the only explanation because users still need to know what is wrong. Client rules are not trusted security checks: the server must repeat every business and integrity rule and the UI must map server failures back to a useful form or field message.

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormsModule, NgForm } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'account-form',
  imports: [FormsModule],
  template: `
    <form #form="ngForm" (ngSubmit)="save(form)">
      <label for="name">Display name</label>
      <input id="name" name="name" [(ngModel)]="model.name"
        required minlength="2" #name="ngModel"
        [attr.aria-invalid]="name.invalid && (name.touched || form.submitted)"
        aria-describedby="name-error">
      <div id="name-error">
        @if (name.invalid && (name.touched || form.submitted)) {
          @if (name.errors?.['required']) { <p>Enter a display name.</p> }
          @if (name.errors?.['minlength']) { <p>Use at least two characters.</p> }
        }
      </div>
      <button type="submit">Create account</button>
    </form>
  `,
})
class AccountForm {
  model = { name: '' };
  save(form: NgForm) { if (form.valid) console.log(this.model); }
}
```

Template-driven and reactive forms are two ways to create Angular's form model. A template-driven form uses `FormsModule`, named `NgModel` directives, and validation attributes. Angular discovers the controls from the template, and two-way binding often connects them to a mutable component object.

A reactive form uses `ReactiveFormsModule` and creates `FormControl`, `FormGroup`, or `FormArray` objects explicitly in TypeScript. The template connects elements to that existing model. Control values and validation state are available synchronously, and tests can exercise the form model without first rendering the template.

The same email field shows the difference:

```typescript
// Template-driven: <input name="email" [(ngModel)]="email" required>
email = '';

// Reactive: <input [formControl]="emailControl">
emailControl = new FormControl('', { nonNullable: true, validators: Validators.required });
```

Template-driven forms are often the clearest option for a short contact or settings form with stable fields. Reactive forms are usually easier when controls are added dynamically, several fields affect one rule, values need composed observable processing, or form logic needs detailed unit tests. Reactive is more explicit but introduces more TypeScript setup; template-driven is concise but spreads the model across markup and directives. Neither makes client validation trustworthy—both must submit to a server that validates again.

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { FormControl, FormsModule, ReactiveFormsModule, Validators } from '@angular/forms';

@Component({
  selector: 'form-comparison',
  imports: [FormsModule, ReactiveFormsModule],
  template: `
    <h2>Template-driven</h2>
    <input name="templateEmail" [(ngModel)]="templateEmail" required>

    <h2>Reactive</h2>
    <input [formControl]="reactiveEmail">
    <p>Reactive valid: {{ reactiveEmail.valid }}</p>
  `,
})
class FormComparison {
  templateEmail = '';
  reactiveEmail = new FormControl('', {
    nonNullable: true,
    validators: [Validators.required],
  });
}
```
